                    <h1>🚀 IIS Site Publishing Management System (SiteDeploy)</h1>
                    <blockquote>
                        <strong>Summary</strong>: An efficient operations and maintenance tool supporting automated IIS site publishing, multi‑server cluster unified deployment, one‑click automatic backup, and version rollback.
                    </blockquote>

                    <h2 id="features">✨ Features</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>🌍 Multi‑language Support</strong>: Natively supports switching between Simplified Chinese and English to seamlessly fit different teams.</li>
                        <li><strong>📦 Core Publishing</strong>: One‑click packaging, uploading, overwriting, and restarting of IIS sites.</li>
                        <li><strong>🌐 Multi‑machine Coordination</strong>: Supports unified management and distributed deployment for single‑server or multi‑server clusters.</li>
                        <li><strong>🛡️ Security Guarantee</strong>: Built‑in automated backup and rapid version rollback mechanisms, allowing second‑level rollback if publishing fails.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p><img src="./images/pub_1.gif" alt="Publishing Demo"></p>

                    <h2 id="install">🛠️ Installation & Configuration</h2>
                    <h3 id="install-single">1. Single Server Deployment (IIS Mode)</h3>
                    <ol>
                        <li><strong>Download & Extract</strong>: Download the master node installation package <code>SiteDeploy.zip</code> and extract it to <code>C:\inetpub\wwwroot\SiteDeploy</code>.</li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Configure Application Pool</strong>:
                            <ul>
                                <li>Name: <code>sitedeploy</code></li>
                                <li>.NET CLR Version: <code>No Managed Code</code></li>
                                <li>Managed Pipeline Mode: <code>Integrated</code></li>
                                <li>Advanced Settings: Change <strong>Identity</strong> from <code>ApplicationPoolIdentity</code> to <strong><code>LocalSystem</code></strong>.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Add Website</strong>:
                            <ul>
                                <li>Site name: <code>SiteDeploy</code></li>
                                <li>Application pool: Select <code>sitedeploy</code></li>
                                <li>Physical path: <code>C:\inetpub\wwwroot\SiteDeploy</code></li>
                                <li>Port number: Custom (e.g., <code>8080</code>)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Access & Initialization</strong>:
                            <ul>
                                <li>Access URL: <code>http://localhost:8080</code></li>
                                <li>
                                    <strong>Login Credentials</strong>:
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>Username: <code>admin</code></li>
                                        <li>Initial Password: Check the <code>admin_initial_password.txt</code> file in the site root directory.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Configuration File Description (<code>appsettings.json</code>)</strong>:
                            <ul>
                                <li><code>SystemConfig.Urls</code>: Configures the URL addresses of slave nodes.</li>
                                <li><code>SystemConfig.IpWhitelist</code>: IP whitelist to restrict access.</li>
                                <li><code>BindUrl</code>: The URL address bound to the current site (required if configured as a Windows service; can be ignored for IIS sites).</li>
                                <li><code>BasePath</code>: The base path of the site.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ol>

                    <h3 id="install-multi">2. Multi‑Server Deployment (IIS Mode)</h3>
                    <blockquote><strong>Prerequisite</strong>: Please ensure that the master node has been successfully deployed and is running.</blockquote>
                    <ol>
                        <li><strong>Download & Extract</strong>: Download the slave node installation package <code>SiteDeploy.Node.zip</code> and extract it to <code>C:\inetpub\wwwroot\SiteDeploy.Node</code>.</li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Configure Application Pool</strong>:
                            <ul>
                                <li>Name: <code>sitedeploy.node</code></li>
                                <li>.NET CLR Version: <code>No Managed Code</code></li>
                                <li>Managed Pipeline Mode: <code>Integrated</code></li>
                                <li>Advanced Settings: Change <strong>Identity</strong> from <code>ApplicationPoolIdentity</code> to <strong><code>LocalSystem</code></strong>.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Add Website</strong>:
                            <ul>
                                <li>Site name: <code>SiteDeploy.Node</code></li>
                                <li>Application pool: Select <code>sitedeploy.node</code></li>
                                <li>Physical path: <code>C:\inetpub\wwwroot\SiteDeploy.Node</code></li>
                                <li>Port number: Custom (e.g., <code>8081</code>)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Verify Slave Node</strong>:
                            <p>Visit <code>http://localhost:8081</code>. If the page properly displays the current site's URL, the configuration is successful (slave nodes do not require login).</p>
                            <p><strong>Configuration File Description (<code>appsettings.json</code>)</strong>:</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><code>SystemConfig.IpWhitelist</code>: IP whitelist restrictions.</li>
                                <li><code>BindUrl</code>: Binding address for Windows service mode (can be ignored for IIS mode).</li>
                                <li><code>BasePath</code>: Site base path.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Associate Master Node</strong>:
                            <p>In the master node's configuration file (<code>appsettings.json</code>), add the slave node URL to <code>SystemConfig.Urls</code> (e.g., <code>http://localhost:8081</code>).</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>

                    <h3 id="install-proxy">3. Reverse Proxy Configuration (High‑Security Environment)</h3>
                    <blockquote><strong>Applicable Scenario</strong>: In high‑security environments where it is inconvenient to switch the application pool identity to <code>LocalSystem</code>, you can run it via a reverse proxy.</blockquote>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Modify Configuration File (<code>appsettings.json</code>)</strong>:
                            <ul>
                                <li>Set the reverse proxy address <code>BindUrl</code> (e.g., <code>http://127.0.0.1:5002</code>).</li>
                                <li>Set the site base path <code>BasePath</code> (e.g., <code>sitedeploy</code>).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Install and Start Windows Service</strong>:
                            <p>Run the <code>install‑service.bat</code> script in the root directory as an administrator.</p>
                            <p>Ensure the browser can successfully access <code>http://127.0.0.1:5002/sitedeploy</code>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Configure IIS Reverse Proxy</strong>:
                            <p>Ensure IIS has the reverse proxy modules installed (<strong>URL Rewrite</strong> + <strong>Application Request Routing (ARR)</strong>).</p>
                            <p>Create an empty directory: <code>C:\inetpub\wwwroot\SiteDeploy‑Proxy</code>.</p>
                            <p>Add a new website in IIS, bind a port (e.g., <code>8080</code>), and point the physical path to the proxy directory created above.</p>
                            <p>Replace the site's <code>web.config</code> file content:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
&lt;configuration&gt;
  &lt;system.webServer&gt;
    &lt;rewrite&gt;
      &lt;rules&gt;
        &lt;rule name="ReverseProxyInboundRule1" stopProcessing="true"&gt;
          &lt;match url="(.*)" /&gt;
          &lt;action type="Rewrite" url="http://127.0.0.1:5002/sitedeploy/{R:1}" /&gt;
        &lt;/rule&gt;
      &lt;/rules&gt;
    &lt;/rewrite&gt;
  &lt;/system.webServer&gt;
&lt;/configuration&gt;</code></pre>
                        </li>
                        <li><strong>Access the System</strong>: Visit <code>http://localhost:8080</code> in your browser to use the system.</li>
                    </ol>

                    <h2 id="usage">💡 Usage Guide</h2>
                    <h3 id="login">System Login</h3>
                    <blockquote>⚠️ <strong>Note</strong>: Login is handled uniformly on the master node; slave nodes do not provide a login interface.</blockquote>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Default Account</strong>: <code>admin</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Default Password</strong>: Found in the <code>admin_initial_password.txt</code> file located in the site root directory.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3 id="perm">Permission Management</h3>
                    <p>After logging in, the administrator can create sub‑users and configure fine‑grained permissions, specifying which servers and specific sites each user can operate.</p>
                    <p><strong>Configuration Example</strong>: Allowing <code>user1</code> to operate <code>http://localhost:5000</code> (crm‑01, crm‑02 sites) and <code>http://localhost:5001</code> (app01, crm‑01, crm‑02 sites).</p>
                    <p><img src="./images/per_1.gif" alt="Permission Management Configuration"></p>

                    <h3 id="cases">Use Cases</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Single Site Publishing</strong>
                            <p><img src="./images/pub_2.gif" alt="Single Site Publishing"></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Multi‑Site Collaborative Publishing</strong>
                            <p><img src="./images/pub_3.gif" alt="Multi‑Site Publishing"></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Rapid Version Rollback/Restoration</strong> (taking <code>http://localhost:5000</code> and <code>http://localhost:5001</code> as examples)
                            <p><img src="./images/pub_4.gif" alt="Version Rollback"></p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
